        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
One of the important concerns of 'late effects' after space radiation exposure is the onset of cancers. The onset of cancer could be initiated by radiation-induced molecular changes that are persistently maintained through a positive feedback cycle and amplified over time. Investigators proposed that two key proteins, nuclear factor kappa Beta (NF-kB) and tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-a), sequentially co-ordinate and control this cascade of events. A novel role of these proteins in radiation-induced tumor development is identified through recent in vitro and in vivo studies after low linear energy transfer (LET) radiation exposure. In this project investigators addressed: (i) how low doses of high LET radiation trigger initial damage signals that remain persistent and are maintained in the memory of the cell/tissue, (ii) how these signals are amplified, (iii) how these amplified signals are transmitted to result in a sustained radial expansion of initial damage, and finally (iv) how radial communication of these signals activates factors that are responsible for predisposing the susceptible cells to carcinogenesis. Investigators aim to examine these objectives using both in vitro (parallel plate flow model) and ex-vivo (mouse-artery organ culture) approaches, in which irradiated normal vascular endothelial cells in an intact tissue setup or organ culture will be allowed to communicate with the co-existing non-targeted normal or pro-tumorigenic cells from lung, skin, and breast tissue. <p>
This study had the following specific aims:<ol>
<li>Study the significance of low doses (0, 0.2, 0.4, or 0.8 Gy) of high-LET radiation exposure on an altered autocrine signaling mechanism that initiates, amplifies, and sustains a positive feedback cycle, i.e., triggering NF-kB -->TNF-a --> NF-kB signaling in the targeted cells. 
<li>Study and compare the significance of the intercellular communication that is responsible for amplification of the signals by replicating the activation of NF-kB - TNF-a positive feedback loop in the non-targeted bystander cells. 
<li>Study, in targeted versus non-targeted bystander cells, the influence of sustained amplification of NF-kB - TNF-a positive feedback loop on (a) stimulating predisposing factors for carcinogenesis and (b) potentiating in vitro and in vivo tumorigenesis. </ol>
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Experiments were conducted with pro-tumorigenic cells (BEP2D, and MCF-10A) that were either irradiated with 56Fe or 28Si (600 MeV/amu) to a dose of 0.2 Gy x 3 or mock irradiated. The cells were repeatedly cultured to 30 to 40 passages after radiation exposure and injected into nude mice to determine the tumorigenic potentials. Similarly, the cells pre-incubated with NF-kB inhibitor, isohelanin, for 30 minutes prior to exposure were also, in parallel, cultured to subsequent passages. Cells at a density of 10 x 10(6) cells in 0.2 ml saline were injected into nude mice. In a second set of experiments, the pro-tumorigenic cells were irradiated and co-cultured with non-irradiated pro-tumorigenic cells. Non-targeted pro-tumorigenic cells that are co-cultured with the irradiated tumor cells were continuously passaged to 30-40 passages. The later experiments were conducted in order to identify whether the TNF-a secreted out from the irradiated cells are responsible for initiating a similar TNF-a-NF-kB feedback cycle in the bystander cells and those cells are capable of transforming into tumorigenic cells. A total of 5 nude mice (Foxn1nu/Foxn1nu) at a 6-8 weeks of age per group was used in this study. 
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The first set of results indicated that the pro-tumorigenic cells co-cultured with irradiated cells were capable of transforming into tumorigenic cells. Both group of mice, i.e., mice either receiving cells that were directly irradiated or mice that were injected with bystander cells that were co-cultured with irradiated cells, showed palpable tumors on day 5. 
<p>
No datasets exist for this study. A final report was archived.
<p>
